{"id":"3acfe479-a1ca-45d8-90cb-50d15dbface8","object":"chat.completion","created":1785027910,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    children = {}\\n    node_map = {}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is None:\\n            root_id = nid\\n        else:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    retained = []\\n    visit_order = []\\n    def dfs(nid):\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] < threshold:\\n            return\\n        retained.append(nid)\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\\n            dfs(child_id)\\n    dfs(root_id)\\n    return {'retained_node_ids': retained, 'visit_order': visit_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that takes a payload object (which is a dict with \"nodes\" (list of node dicts) and \"utility_threshold\" (float) and possibly other fields like \"_case_nonce\"). The task is not fully specified beyond the output schema. The schema requires \"retained_node_ids\" and \"visit_order\" as arrays of strings. From the fixture payloads, we have nodes with properties: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility.\n\nThe task seems to be about selecting/navigating a tree. The output has \"retained_node_ids\" (which nodes are kept) and \"visit_order\" (order visited). The problem likely involves pruning or traversing nodes based on utility threshold. Because we have a \"utility_threshold\" in the payload.\n\nLooking at the examples: In first case, threshold 0.55. Nodes: root (1.0), n1 (0.941), n2 (0.742), n3 (0.760), n4 (0.208), n5 (0.021), n6 (0.352). Retained nodes? Possibly those with utility >= threshold? root (1.0 >= 0.55), n1 (0.941), n2 (0.742), n3 (0.760) all >= 0.55; n4 (0.208 <), n5 (0.021 <), n6 (0.352 <). So retained would be 4 nodes. Visit order? Could be a traversal (DFS, BFS) from root, visiting only retained nodes, or visiting all nodes but reporting in order? The output arrays are strings (node IDs). Without more spec, we need to infer the algorithm.\n\nMaybe it's about removing nodes that are \"removable\" and have utility below threshold, but also considering their children? Or it could be about computing a \"pruned tree\" where we remove removable nodes with utility below threshold, and then traverse the remaining tree in some order. Also note \"visit_order\" suggests an ordering, possibly post-order or pre-order.\n\nLet's examine the second fixture: threshold 0.55, nodes: root(1.0), n1(0.532<0.55), n2(0.954), n3(0.842), n4(0.904), n5(0.032), n6(0.499). So root and n2,n3,n4 >= threshold; n1,n5,n6 below. n1 is removable? No, removable=false for n1. n6 is removable true. n4 is removable true but utility above. So retained nodes might be those that are either not removable, or have utility >= threshold. But n1 is not removable but utility below threshold, would it be retained? Possibly yes (since not removable). Similarly n5 is not removable (false) but utility below, retained? So that would give root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5 as retained? That seems many. But then visit_order?\n\nMaybe the problem is about a \"utility-based pruning\" where we only keep nodes that are \"valuable enough\" or that are necessary ancestors. Could be like: start from root, traverse depth-first. When encountering a node, if its utility is below threshold and it's removable, we prune it and its subtree. If not removable, we keep it and visit its children. Also, ancestors are always kept if they lead to a kept child? Root always kept because parent null and not removable. So retained_node_ids would be all nodes that are not pruned. visit_order would be the order of visitation (e.g., DFS pre-order).\n\nLet's simulate on first case with DFS pre-order:\n- root: utility 1.0 >= 0.55 -> keep, visit order: root\n- children: n1 (depth1, removable false, utility 0.941 >=0.55) -> keep, visit: n1\n- children of n1: n6 (depth2, removable true, utility 0.352 <0.55) -> since removable and below threshold, prune n6 (and its children none). So n6 not retained.\n- back to root, next child: n2 (removable true, utility 0.742 >=0.55) -> keep, visit: n2\n- child of n2: n4 (removable true, utility 0.208 <0.55) -> prune. So n4 not retained.\n- back to root, next child: n3 (removable false, utility 0.760 >=0.55) -> keep, visit: n3\n- child of n3: n5 (removable false, utility 0.021 <0.55) -> removable false, so even below threshold, we keep it (since not removable). So n5 retained and visited.\n- So retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n5. That's 5 nodes. Visit order: root, n1, n2, n3, n5 (DFS pre-order). Check if that matches expected? No expected given, but plausible.\n\nSecond case: root (1.0) keep, n1 (removable false, utility 0.532 <0.55) -> not removable so keep, visit n1. child of n1: n6 (removable true, utility 0.499<0.55) -> prune. Back to root: n2 (removable true, utility 0.954>=0.55) keep, visit n2. child n4 (removable true, utility 0.904>=0.55) keep, visit n4. Back to n2 no more children. root: n3 (removable false, utility 0.842>=0.55) keep, visit n3. child n5 (removable false, utility 0.032<0.55) -> not removable, keep, visit n5. So retained: root, n1, n2, n4, n3, n5 (6 nodes). Visit order: root, n1, n2, n4, n3, n5.\n\nThird case: threshold 0.55, nodes: root(1.0), n1(0.025<, not removable), n2(0.376<, removable true), n3(0.262<, not removable), n4(0.576>=, removable true), n5(0.916>=, not removable), n6(0.631>=, removable true). DFS from root: root keep. n1: not removable, utility below threshold -> keep (since not removable). But children: n6 (removable true, utility 0.631>=0.55) -> keep. So n1 and n6 kept. Then root->n2: removable true, utility 0.376<0.55 -> prune n2 and its subtree (n4). So n2 not retained, n4 not retained. root->n3: not removable, utility 0.262<0.55 -> keep. child n5: not removable, utility 0.916>=0.55 -> keep. So retained: root, n1, n6, n3, n5. Visit order: root, n1, n6, n3, n5.\n\nThat seems consistent with the rule: When visiting a node:\n- If the node's utility >= utility_threshold, or its 'removable' is False, then include it and recursively process its children (if any). But if removable is True and utility < threshold, then skip the node and its entire subtree (do not visit children). The ancestor nodes are visited regardless? Actually, the decision to include a node might also depend on whether it is an ancestor of a kept node? The rule above ensures that all nodes visited are either kept or pruned. But note that if a node is not removable and below threshold, we keep it and still visit its children. That seems correct from the simulation.\n\nBut there is nuance: root is always kept (removable false, utility high). Also, the visit order is the order in which we \"visit\" nodes in a depth-first pre-order traversal of the tree, but we only visit nodes that are not pruned, and we \"visit\" in the sense of adding to visit_order when we first encounter the node (pre-order). The retained_node_ids are all nodes that are not pruned (i.e., visited and included). In the algorithm, if we prune a node (removable and utility low), we do not add it to retained or visit_order, and we do not traverse its children.\n\nThus solve(case) should return {\"retained_node_ids\": [ ... ], \"visit_order\": [...] }.\n\nWe need to implement this in Python. The function receives a dict `case` which contains keys: \"nodes\" (list of dicts) and \"utility_threshold\" (float). There may be other keys like \"_case_nonce\" which we ignore. We need to parse nodes, build a tree (adjacency list or mapping parent->children), then perform DFS from the root node (where parent is null). The root's id is \"root\"? Not necessarily; we