 an object with key \"implementation\". The entire response must be exactly that JSON object.\n\nSo final answer:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    \\n    # Build children list preserving order\\n    children = {}\\n    node_map = {}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is None:\\n            root_id = nid\\n        else:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    \\n    retained = []\\n    visit_order = []\\n    \\n    def dfs(nid):\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        # Decision: if removable and utility below threshold, prune\\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] < threshold:\\n            return\\n        retained.append(nid)\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        # Process children in order\\n        for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\\n            dfs(child_id)\\n    \\n    dfs(root_id)\\n    return {'retained_node_ids': retained, 'visit_order': visit_order}\"}\n\nBut need to ensure proper escaping: backslashes, quotes, newlines as \\n. Since we output JSON, we must encode the Python source string with escaping. In our response, we will produce a JSON object with \\n and \\\". \n\nLet's write the Python code as a string with proper indentation. Then we'll put it into a JSON object.\n\nWe could also use a non-recursive stack to avoid recursion limit, but recursion depth is small (depth up to number of nodes). Standard library only, fine.\n\nMake sure to handle the case where some nodes might not have children (empty list). Also note: the 'removable' field is boolean.\n\nCheck: In first simulation, we retained root, n1, n2, n3, n5. That includes n5 which is below threshold but not removable. That's consistent.\n\nNow produce final JSON.\n\nLet's format the code nicely:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    children = {}\n    node_map = {}\n    root_id = None\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_map[nid] = node\n        parent = node['parent']\n        if parent is None:\n            root_id = nid\n        else:\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n    retained = []\n    visit_order = []\n    def dfs(nid):\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] < threshold:\n            return\n        retained.append(nid)\n        visit_order.append(nid)\n        for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\n            dfs(child_id)\n    dfs(root_id)\n    return {'retained_node_ids': retained, 'visit_order': visit_order}\n\nNow encode as JSON string. We'll need to escape backslashes and quotes. For example, newline becomes \\n, quote becomes \\\", backslash becomes \\\\.\n\nWe can do that manually or rely on json.dumps. Since I'm writing text, I'll manually escape to be safe.\n\nI'll write the implementation value as a string with proper escapes.\n\nLet me construct the JSON:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    children = {}\\n    node_map = {}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is None:\\n            root_id = nid\\n        else:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    retained = []\\n    visit_order = []\\n    def dfs(nid):\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] < threshold:\\n            return\\n        retained.append(nid)\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\\n            dfs(child_id)\\n    dfs(root_id)\\n    return {'retained_node_ids': retained, 'visit_order': visit_order}\"}\n\nBut need to escape single quotes inside the string? In JSON, double quotes delimit the string, so single quotes are fine as literals. However, we have single quotes in the Python code: case['nodes'] etc. That's fine; they don't need escaping. But we have double quotes in the Python code? The Python code uses double quotes for the strings passed to the return dict? Actually, the return uses single quotes: {'retained_node_ids': retained, 'visit_order': visit_order}. So no double quotes. But we have double quotes in the outer JSON. So no conflict.\n\nBut there is a backslash in the newline \\n and also in the function definition newline. We have to represent newlines as \\n. Also backslashes for indentation? No, just newline. Also if there was a literal backslash in the code, we'd need to double it, but there isn't.\n\nSo the above string is acceptable. However, we must ensure that the entire response is a valid JSON object. Let me check for any unescaped control characters. The newlines are escaped as \\n. Good.\n\nThus final."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1858,"completion_tokens":3821,"total_tokens":5679,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3596},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":66},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}